 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "cyprus_selimiye",
   "title": "Selimiye Mosque",
   "text": "This was the Cathedral of Saint Sophia, a Gothic church built by the Lusignan kings of Cyprus between the 1200s and the 1300s, where they were crowned. After the Ottoman conquest of 1570 it became a mosque and the two minarets went up on its towers. It was named the Selimiye in 1954, after Sultan Selim II. A French cathedral with minarets: Nicosia in one building."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "cyprus_bedesten",
   "title": "The Bedesten",
   "text": "Next to the mosque stands the Bedesten, a church of Byzantine and then Gothic times which the Ottomans turned into a bedesten, a covered market for cloth and valuables. It has been restored, and the stone carving over its north door is worth the crick in your neck."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "cyprus_buyuk_han",
   "title": "The Büyük Han",
   "text": "The Büyük Han, the Great Inn, was built by the Ottomans in 1572, soon after they took the island: two storeys of rooms round a courtyard, where travelling merchants slept upstairs and their animals and goods stayed below. A small mosque stands on pillars over the fountain in the middle. Today it's cafes and craft shops."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "cyprus_kyrenia_gate",
   "title": "The Kyrenia Gate",
   "text": "The Kyrenia Gate is one of the gates in the Venetian walls, built in the 1560s on the road north over the mountains to Kyrenia, Girne in Turkish. The Ottomans later put a little domed room on top. Today the road goes round it and the gate stands on its own, like a man who's been left holding a door."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "cyprus_lira",
   "title": "The Lira",
   "text": "The north uses the Turkish lira. It has lost a great deal of its value in recent years, so prices are big numbers and they change often, and many shops will quote in euros or pounds as well. Check which money a price is in before you nod."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "cyprus_north_1983",
   "title": "Northern Cyprus",
   "text": "In 1983 Turkish Cypriot leaders declared the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus. Only Turkey recognises it as a state; the UN and nearly every other country treat the whole island as the Republic of Cyprus. On the ground, Turkish Cypriots run their own affairs here, with their own parliament, police and passports, and Turkish troops are still stationed in the north."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "cyprus_turkish_cypriots",
   "title": "Turkish Cypriots",
   "text": "Turkish-speaking Cypriots have lived on the island since the Ottoman conquest of 1570 and 1571. Before the 1960s they lived all over Cyprus, in mixed villages and their own. From 1963 many lived in enclaves behind barricades, and after 1974 most moved north, as most Greek Cypriots in the north moved south. Both communities lost homes, neighbours and a way of life."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "cyprus_missing",
   "title": "The Missing",
   "text": "Since 1981 the Committee on Missing Persons, with Greek Cypriot and Turkish Cypriot members working side by side, has searched for the people from both communities who went missing in the 1960s and in 1974. Its scientists have identified many hundreds and returned them to their families. It is quiet, slow work, and some of the most trusted on the island."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "cyprus_hellim",
   "title": "Hellim",
   "text": "On this side the cheese is hellim, the same cheese the south calls halloumi: sheep and goat's milk, mint and brine. It's protected by the EU under both names, which is the island in a nutshell, or a cheese rind."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "LEFKOŞA / NICOSIA. The crossing to the south is behind you. ARASTA and the SELİMİYE, straight on."
  }
 ],
